dtd: two races in the tracking of tile users

1. ABA on tile->last_user. A completed reader stays referenced by
   tile->last_user after its task structure has gone back to the mempool.
   When a new task of the same class reuses that memory and accesses the
   tile on a later flow, the "same task uses this tile on several flows"
   test in parsec_insert_dtd_task (parent not alive branch) matches by
   pointer and releases a reader on the data copy of another flow. The
   readers counter then drops to zero early and the next writer passes the
   WAR gate while a reader is still running (wrong results; with asserts,
   "parsec_dtd_data_copy_reader_release: previous > 0").
   Only trust the match when a previous flow of the task uses the tile.

2. Remote writer after a remote writer with local readers in between.
   The second writer is a successor in the chain that the iteration of the
   first writer walks when its data arrives, but it is only retained while
   it is the last writer. If the data of the first writer arrives after a
   third writer has been inserted, the iteration reaches a released task
   (segfault/deadlock in made_sure_nextinline_is_null). Retain it as when
   the previous writer is local, and release it when the iteration
   reaches it.

